      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-280400",
      ":question/problem": "If $W=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =5|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(4\\cdot 1-6\\cdot (-3))+2((-1)(1)-6\\cdot 2)+3((-1)(-3)-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =5(4+18)+2(-1-12)+3(3-8) \\\\  & =5(22)+2(-13)+3(-5) \\\\  & =110-26-15 \\\\  & =69\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
